{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"async-future","title":"async, future & promise","group":"Concurrency","order":74,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>std::async</code> runs a function (possibly on another thread) and hands back a <code>std::future</code> — a handle to a result that will be available later. Exceptions travel through the future too.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-track-next\"></i><code>std::async</code> &amp; <code>std::future</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;future&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;numeric&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nlong long sumRange(const std::vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; v, std::size_t from, std::size_t to) {\n    return std::accumulate(v.begin() + from, v.begin() + to, 0LL);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v(10'000);\n    std::iota(v.begin(), v.end(), 1);\n\n    auto half = v.size() / 2;\n    std::future&lt;long long&gt; first = std::async(std::launch::async, sumRange, std::cref(v), 0, half);\n    long long second = sumRange(v, half, v.size());            // meanwhile, work on this thread\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; first.get() + second &lt;&lt; '\\n';                 // get() waits, then returns the value\n\n    auto failing = std::async(std::launch::async, [] () -&gt; int { throw std::runtime_error(\"task failed\"); });\n    try { failing.get(); }                                     // the exception is rethrown here\n    catch (const std::exception&amp; e) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"caught: \" &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>50005000\ncaught: task failed</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Launch policy</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>std::launch::async</code></td><td>Runs on a new thread immediately</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::launch::deferred</code></td><td>Runs lazily on the thread that calls <code>get()</code>/<code>wait()</code></td></tr><tr><td>default (<code>async | deferred</code>)</td><td>Implementation chooses — pass <code>std::launch::async</code> if you need real concurrency</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>The future returned by <code>std::async</code> <strong>blocks in its destructor</strong> until the task finishes. <code>std::async(f);</code> without keeping the future therefore runs synchronously — a common surprise.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-send\"></i><code>std::promise</code> — setting a future by hand</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;future&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;thread&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::promise&lt;std::string&gt; promise;\n    std::future&lt;std::string&gt; result = promise.get_future();\n\n    std::jthread worker([p = std::move(promise)]() mutable {\n        p.set_value(\"downloaded 42 KB\");                  // fulfils the future (once)\n    });\n\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; result.get() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>downloaded 42 KB</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-package\"></i><code>std::packaged_task</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;future&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;thread&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::packaged_task&lt;int(int, int)&gt; task([](int a, int b) { return a * b; });   // wraps a callable\n    std::future&lt;int&gt; f = task.get_future();\n    std::jthread t(std::move(task), 6, 7);                // run it anywhere — e.g. a thread pool\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; f.get() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>42</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Who produces the value</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>std::async</code></td><td>The library runs your function</td><td>Fire off a computation, collect the result</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::packaged_task</code></td><td>Whoever invokes the task</td><td>Task queues / thread pools</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::promise</code></td><td>Your code calls <code>set_value</code>/<code>set_exception</code></td><td>Results produced by callbacks or events</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::shared_future</code></td><td>—</td><td>Several threads waiting on the same result</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What's the difference between std::thread and std::async?</summary><div><p><code>std::thread</code> just runs a function; getting a result or an exception back requires your own shared state and synchronisation. <code>std::async</code> returns a <code>std::future</code> that delivers the return value — or rethrows the exception — when you call <code>get()</code>, and with the default policy may not even create a thread.</p></div></details></section>"}